# AUTHOR-PAREN PRESERVATION — the SPECULATION/BACKTRACK cell.
#
# `Parser::try_parse` restores `self.pos` on a failed speculation, and
# `parse_expr` is reachable UNDER speculation (generic-type-args → `parse_type`
# → array-size `parse_expr`). A paren recorded inside an abandoned attempt is a
# PHANTOM: the fallback parse records the same paren again, and the layer count
# grows every pass. Measured on this shape before the truncate landed:
# 3× → 9× → 27× parens per pass, with `fmt_output_reparses_corpus_wide` GREEN
# throughout — no corpus file has this shape.
#
# The fix is the chokepoint: `try_parse`'s `None` arm truncates the push-log to
# its saved length, so an abandoned speculation leaves nothing behind.
#
# ⚠ TEXT-ONLY, deliberately, and NOT wired to build or run. `int[N]` as a
# generic argument is a LIVE, PRE-EXISTING defect of its own (check passes,
# debug ICEs, release miscompiles) filed separately — building this file would
# red an unrelated gate with a panic that has nothing to do with parens, and
# quietly swapping the shape would bury that defect instead. The RUNNABLE
# fingerprint of the same mechanism, using a generic callee that resolves, is
# `speculation_run.gg`.
#
# RED (a build without the `try_parse` truncate): the parens multiply on each
# pass. Green on the pre-fix binary only in the sense that it deletes them.

void main():
    Vector[int] buf = [1, 2, 3]
    int a = buf[int[(2)]]
    print(a)
